&gt; It's "type safety" in that it permits the programmer to, without ceremony, explicitly bind values that doesn't have semantic value (float) to something that does (lat=).

i guess "type safe" in scare quotes means something entirely different from type safe without them, then. i'd describe that as having a type system at all.

&gt; Lat and long should be different types, but plenty of APIs don't make that distinction, resulting in a plethora of function calls that should be rejected by a well typed program but aren't because someone failed to make a newtype

well they should be if you want a well typed program and consider them to be different types.

&gt; Creating a function that accepts a struct enforces that binding

no it doesn't, and neither does named parameters. if you model two values of distinct types as values of the same type, nothing in the language will ever enforce that you're passing a latitude as `lat` or a longitude as `long`. as far as type safety goes, all is fine as long as you are passing values of the types specified. it doesn't matter whether you name them or what you name them. `recordPerson(name: str, yearOfBirth: i64)` won't know whether `name` is actually an acceptable name unless you enforce that *outside* the type system or define a type that encapsulates the properties you are looking for. whether you are calling that by `recordPerson("';alert('PENIS')", -9223372036854775808)` or `recordPerson(name="';alert('PENIS')", yearOfBirth=-9223372036854775808)` makes absolutely no difference to that end.

the phrase you are looking for is not "enforces" but something like "reminds the programmer of". that'll always be the case for as long as the types in your head are different from the types you actually encode in the program. again, i'm not arguing against the idea that named parameters are useful or make errors like this less likely to happen, just that it has absolutely nothing to do with type safety and that using structs to the same effect or not using structs when you don't is not a ton of effort.

