> This isn't any more error prone than missing or misplaced end braces.

A missing end brace is first of all a syntax error. It won't slip past compile, much less a test. A misplaced brace may be an issue, but is always a consequence of a programmer explicitly spelling it out in the wrong place.

Here are a couple of examples:

diff a:

     void foo(void)
     {
         if (x)
    -    {
             y();
    -        z();
    -    }
     }

diff b:

     void foo(void)
     {
         if (x)
         {
             y();
    -        z();
    +        bar();
         }
     }

Merge both of these in order and enjoy the bug silently slipping by without conflict. Wouldn't have happened if the braces were kept.

Next, let's quickly smoke test our z function.

    void foo(void)
    {
        if (x)
            y();
        z();
    }

I'll comment the y call out because it's been tested before, has no effect on state it shares with z, is irrelevant to what I've just implemented and needs a full test environment on Jenkins to run anyway. Test passes fine. I re-introduce the commented out section, and the code breaks during actual testing because z was secretly also dependent on x, a problem that never materialized during the smoke test because my commenting y made the z call conditional.

Neither of these problems happen when you consistently always use braces. That said, I think GCC with all warnings enabled will lint for misleading indentation, which would catch these. This is is a behavior you can't rely on if you use an automatic code formatter (say clang-format) because the formatter will auto indent the misleadingly indented sections. So that's a loss right there; can't use autoformatters and therefore can't conveniently maintain a consistent style.

> Not using the braces for single line statements actually informs you that it was intentional* and it is not a missing or misplaced end brace.

The presence of a beginning brace and the lack of a matching end brace is a 100% indicator of a missing end brace.

> *So long as that's the standard practice in the codebase, and it is for us.

A consistent coding style is preferable to an inconsistent one. That IMO is orthogonal to the issue of omitting or including braces for conditional statements.

> In the end, it's an intentional feature of the language, we got used to using it and have actualized multiple benefits to doing so. You don't have to like it, believe it, or start doing it.

In some languages conditional simple statements have been omitted because they are generally considered bad practice and are a common source of both errors and unnecessary changes when they eventually need to encompass multiple statements. It's intentional in others, sure, but the justification is usually either to be C-like or to simplify parsing rules (so that blocks are just a type of statement). Whether I like it, believe it or do it is irrelevant here. What I'd like to know is a compelling reason to omit them. You say that you have actualized many benefits by those means, but the only one you've mentioned so far is the vague notion that the omission looks intentional. How it looks more intentional than explicitly spelling the beginning and end of the conditional code out using braces, I can't figure out on my own.

I've also pointed out a real life case of where this caused a significant problem. The Apple "goto fail" SSL bug is remarkable in how utterly trivial the problem is and how easy the fix is.

